1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,699
Welcome to Calculus. In this first lesson,
we'll pin down the idea everything else is

2
00:00:05,699 --> 00:00:07,040
built on: the limit.

3
00:00:08,167 --> 00:00:13,736
Calculus studies change at an instant,
like a speedometer reading. But speed is

4
00:00:13,736 --> 00:00:17,191
distance over time, and an instant has
zero time.

5
00:00:17,641 --> 00:00:22,483
Limits let us ask what a ratio approaches,
without dividing by zero.

6
00:00:23,633 --> 00:00:30,109
The limit of f of x as x approaches c
equals L means f of x can be made as close

7
00:00:30,109 --> 00:00:35,452
to L as we like by taking x close enough
to c, but not equal to c.

8
00:00:35,902 --> 00:00:38,014
The value at c itself is ignored.

9
00:00:39,133 --> 00:00:44,701
Estimate the limit as x approaches zero of
sine x over x. Make a table.

10
00:00:45,151 --> 00:00:50,669
At point one, point nine nine eight three.
At point zero one, point nine nine nine

11
00:00:50,669 --> 00:00:54,303
nine eight. From the left, the same. The
limit is one.

12
00:00:55,433 --> 00:00:58,527
Graphs show limits as the height the curve
heads toward.

13
00:00:58,977 --> 00:01:04,630
The graph of sine x over x has a hole at
zero, but both sides head to height one.

14
00:01:05,767 --> 00:01:09,031
We write lim, x arrow c, f of x equals L.

15
00:01:09,481 --> 00:01:14,750
Tables and graphs give evidence, not
proof. Soon we'll compute limits exactly.

16
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:22,068
Your turn. Use a table to estimate the
limit as x approaches zero of e to the x

17
00:01:22,068 --> 00:01:23,473
minus one, over x.

18
00:01:23,923 --> 00:01:25,459
Pause the video and try it.

19
00:01:25,909 --> 00:01:32,038
At point zero one, about one point zero
zero five; at negative point zero one,

20
00:01:32,038 --> 00:01:35,573
about point nine nine five. The limit is
one.

21
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:40,071
In this lesson, we'll approach a point
from just one side.

22
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:47,284
The left-hand limit uses x values less
than c. The right-hand limit uses x values

23
00:01:47,284 --> 00:01:48,411
greater than c.

24
00:01:48,861 --> 00:01:51,847
A function can approach different heights
from each side.

25
00:01:52,967 --> 00:01:57,669
We write x approaches c from the minus
side for the left, and from the plus side

26
00:01:57,669 --> 00:01:58,492
for the right.

27
00:01:58,942 --> 00:02:04,303
For the floor function at x equals two,
the left limit is one and the right limit

28
00:02:04,303 --> 00:02:04,766
is two.

29
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:11,745
The two-sided limit exists exactly when
both one-sided limits exist and are equal.

30
00:02:12,195 --> 00:02:16,355
For the floor function at two, they
differ, so the limit does not exist.

31
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:23,047
Let f be x squared for x less than one,
and two x plus one for x at least one.

32
00:02:23,497 --> 00:02:28,489
The left limit at one is one; the right
limit is three. No two-sided limit.

33
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:33,793
Find the limits of the absolute value of
x, over x, at zero.

34
00:02:34,243 --> 00:02:39,815
For x greater than zero, it's one. For x
less than zero, it's negative one. So the

35
00:02:39,815 --> 00:02:44,844
right limit is one, the left limit is
negative one, and the limit does not

36
00:02:44,844 --> 00:02:45,251
exist.

37
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:52,528
Your turn. f is three minus x for x less
than two, and x minus one for x at least

38
00:02:52,528 --> 00:02:54,571
two. Find the limit at two.

39
00:02:55,021 --> 00:02:56,557
Pause the video and try it.

40
00:02:57,007 --> 00:02:59,524
Both sides give one, so the limit is one.

41
00:03:00,666 --> 00:03:05,553
In this lesson, we'll learn the rules that
let us compute limits by breaking them

42
00:03:05,553 --> 00:03:06,277
into pieces.

43
00:03:07,399 --> 00:03:12,844
If the limits of f and g exist, the limit
of a sum is the sum of the limits, and the

44
00:03:12,844 --> 00:03:14,205
same for differences.

45
00:03:14,655 --> 00:03:17,001
A constant multiple can come out front.

46
00:03:18,133 --> 00:03:23,740
The limit of a product is the product of
the limits. The limit of a quotient is the

47
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:27,050
quotient, as long as the bottom limit
isn't zero.

48
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,252
These laws turn hard limits into easy
ones.

49
00:03:31,399 --> 00:03:37,088
The limit of f to the n is the limit of f,
to the n. Roots work the same way when

50
00:03:37,088 --> 00:03:37,650
defined.

51
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:42,153
So the limit as x approaches four of the
square root of x is two.

52
00:03:43,299 --> 00:03:48,787
Together, these mean polynomials and
rational functions can be evaluated by

53
00:03:48,787 --> 00:03:52,665
direct substitution, when the denominator
isn't zero.

54
00:03:53,115 --> 00:03:59,670
The limit as x approaches two of x cubed
minus four x plus one, over x plus three,

55
00:03:59,670 --> 00:04:00,709
is one fifth.

56
00:04:01,833 --> 00:04:07,109
Suppose the limit of f is three and the
limit of g is negative two, as x

57
00:04:07,109 --> 00:04:08,062
approaches c.

58
00:04:08,512 --> 00:04:14,562
Then the limit of two f minus g squared is
six minus four, which is two. And the

59
00:04:14,562 --> 00:04:17,813
limit of f over g is negative three
halves.

60
00:04:18,933 --> 00:04:23,882
Your turn. With those same limits, find
the limit of f times g plus five.

61
00:04:24,332 --> 00:04:25,868
Pause the video and try it.

62
00:04:26,318 --> 00:04:30,158
Three times negative two plus five, which
is negative one.

63
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:36,399
In this lesson, we'll handle limits where
substitution gives zero over zero.

64
00:04:37,533 --> 00:04:43,336
Zero over zero is called indeterminate. It
doesn't mean the limit fails; it means we

65
00:04:43,336 --> 00:04:44,787
need to do more work.

66
00:04:45,237 --> 00:04:49,994
The goal is to find an equivalent
expression that we can substitute into.

67
00:04:51,133 --> 00:04:56,691
Factor and cancel. The limit as x
approaches two of x cubed minus eight,

68
00:04:56,691 --> 00:04:58,003
over x minus two.

69
00:04:58,453 --> 00:05:04,363
Factor the top: x minus two times x
squared plus two x plus four. Cancel, then

70
00:05:04,363 --> 00:05:07,925
substitute: four plus four plus four is
twelve.

71
00:05:09,067 --> 00:05:15,401
Multiply by a conjugate for roots. The
limit as x approaches nine of the square

72
00:05:15,401 --> 00:05:18,688
root of x minus three, over x minus nine.

73
00:05:19,138 --> 00:05:25,251
Multiply by the square root of x plus
three. The top becomes x minus nine, which

74
00:05:25,251 --> 00:05:27,543
cancels, leaving one over six.

75
00:05:28,667 --> 00:05:35,505
Combine complex fractions. The limit as h
approaches zero of one over three plus h

76
00:05:35,505 --> 00:05:37,840
minus one third, all over h.

77
00:05:38,290 --> 00:05:44,873
The top is negative h over three times
three plus h. Divide by h and let h go to

78
00:05:44,873 --> 00:05:46,930
zero: negative one ninth.

79
00:05:48,067 --> 00:05:54,039
Two key trig limits: sine x over x
approaches one, and one minus cosine x,

80
00:05:54,039 --> 00:05:57,752
over x, approaches zero, as x approaches
zero.

81
00:05:58,202 --> 00:06:04,837
So the limit of sine five x over x is five
times sine five x over five x, which is

82
00:06:04,837 --> 00:06:05,242
five.

83
00:06:06,367 --> 00:06:12,276
Your turn. Find the limit as x approaches
zero of sine three x over sine two x.

84
00:06:12,726 --> 00:06:14,262
Pause the video and try it.

85
00:06:14,712 --> 00:06:21,230
Rewrite as three halves times sine three x
over three x, times two x over sine two x.

86
00:06:21,230 --> 00:06:23,224
The limit is three halves.

87
00:06:24,366 --> 00:06:29,105
In this lesson, we'll trap a tricky
function between two simpler ones to find

88
00:06:29,105 --> 00:06:29,721
its limit.

89
00:06:30,866 --> 00:06:36,112
If a function is stuck between two others,
and both of those head to the same value,

90
00:06:36,112 --> 00:06:37,735
it has nowhere else to go.

91
00:06:38,185 --> 00:06:41,663
That's the squeeze theorem, sometimes
called the sandwich theorem.

92
00:06:42,799 --> 00:06:49,225
If g of x is at most f of x, which is at
most h of x near c, and g and h both

93
00:06:49,225 --> 00:06:52,229
approach L, then f approaches L too.

94
00:06:52,679 --> 00:06:54,812
The bounds only need to hold near c.

95
00:06:55,933 --> 00:07:02,954
Find the limit as x approaches zero of x
squared sine of one over x. Sine of one

96
00:07:02,954 --> 00:07:05,149
over x oscillates wildly.

97
00:07:05,599 --> 00:07:11,498
But it's between negative one and one, so
the function is between negative x squared

98
00:07:11,498 --> 00:07:15,220
and x squared. Both go to zero, so the
limit is zero.

99
00:07:16,366 --> 00:07:20,825
The squeeze theorem also proves that sine
x over x approaches one.

100
00:07:21,275 --> 00:07:27,883
A geometric argument with the unit circle
shows cosine x is at most sine x over x,

101
00:07:27,883 --> 00:07:32,880
which is at most one. As x goes to zero,
cosine x goes to one.

102
00:07:33,999 --> 00:07:39,645
The trick is choosing bounds. Use the fact
that sine and cosine stay between negative

103
00:07:39,645 --> 00:07:40,442
one and one.

104
00:07:40,892 --> 00:07:47,814
For the limit at infinity of cosine x over
x, bound it between negative one over x

105
00:07:47,814 --> 00:07:50,684
and one over x. The limit is zero.

106
00:07:51,833 --> 00:07:57,998
Your turn. Find the limit as x approaches
zero of x times cosine of one over x.

107
00:07:58,448 --> 00:07:59,984
Pause the video and try it.

108
00:08:00,434 --> 00:08:06,374
It's between negative absolute x and
absolute x, both going to zero. The limit

109
00:08:06,374 --> 00:08:06,983
is zero.

110
00:08:08,133 --> 00:08:13,210
In this lesson, we'll define exactly what
it means for a function to be continuous.

111
00:08:14,333 --> 00:08:20,377
f is continuous at c if three things hold:
f of c is defined, the limit as x

112
00:08:20,377 --> 00:08:24,274
approaches c exists, and the limit equals
f of c.

113
00:08:24,724 --> 00:08:28,586
Informally, you can draw the graph there
without lifting your pencil.

114
00:08:29,733 --> 00:08:35,584
A removable discontinuity is a hole: the
limit exists, but f of c is missing or

115
00:08:35,584 --> 00:08:36,325
different.

116
00:08:36,775 --> 00:08:42,944
x squared minus four over x minus two has
a hole at two; defining f of two as four

117
00:08:42,944 --> 00:08:43,922
would fix it.

118
00:08:45,066 --> 00:08:49,952
A jump discontinuity happens when the left
and right limits exist but differ.

119
00:08:50,402 --> 00:08:53,730
Step functions and many piecewise
functions have jumps.

120
00:08:54,866 --> 00:08:58,408
An infinite discontinuity is a vertical
asymptote.

121
00:08:58,858 --> 00:09:02,378
One over x minus three has one at x equals
three.

122
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:10,044
Find k so that f is continuous: f equals k
x plus one for x less than two, and x

123
00:09:10,044 --> 00:09:12,908
squared minus k for x at least two.

124
00:09:13,358 --> 00:09:19,743
Set the two pieces equal at two: two k
plus one equals four minus k, so three k

125
00:09:19,743 --> 00:09:22,168
equals three and k equals one.

126
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:29,562
Your turn. Classify the discontinuity of x
plus one over x squared minus one at x

127
00:09:29,562 --> 00:09:31,108
equals negative one.

128
00:09:31,558 --> 00:09:33,094
Pause the video and try it.

129
00:09:33,544 --> 00:09:39,717
It simplifies to one over x minus one, so
x equals negative one is a removable

130
00:09:39,717 --> 00:09:41,458
discontinuity, a hole.

131
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:47,923
In this lesson, we'll use continuity to
guarantee that a function hits every value

132
00:09:47,923 --> 00:09:48,637
in between.

133
00:09:49,767 --> 00:09:56,189
If f is continuous on a closed interval
from a to b, and N is any number between f

134
00:09:56,189 --> 00:10:01,436
of a and f of b, then some c in the
interval has f of c equal to N.

135
00:10:01,886 --> 00:10:04,425
A continuous function can't skip values.

136
00:10:05,567 --> 00:10:10,729
Continuity is essential. A function with a
jump can leap right over a value.

137
00:10:11,179 --> 00:10:13,313
So always check continuity first.

138
00:10:14,433 --> 00:10:20,855
Show that x cubed plus x minus one equals
zero has a solution between zero and one.

139
00:10:21,305 --> 00:10:27,481
f of zero is negative one and f of one is
one. The polynomial is continuous, and

140
00:10:27,481 --> 00:10:32,654
zero is between negative one and one, so
there's a root in between.

141
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:39,168
Check the midpoint to narrow it. f of one
half is one eighth plus one half minus

142
00:10:39,168 --> 00:10:41,651
one, which is negative three eighths.

143
00:10:42,101 --> 00:10:47,383
So the root lies between one half and one.
Repeating this is called the bisection

144
00:10:47,383 --> 00:10:47,839
method.

145
00:10:48,967 --> 00:10:53,745
At six in the morning it was forty
degrees, and at noon it was sixty degrees.

146
00:10:54,195 --> 00:10:59,640
Temperature changes continuously, so at
some moment in between, it was exactly

147
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:00,617
fifty degrees.

148
00:11:01,767 --> 00:11:07,015
Your turn. Does cosine x equal x somewhere
between zero and pi over two?

149
00:11:07,465 --> 00:11:09,001
Pause the video and try it.

150
00:11:09,451 --> 00:11:17,591
Let g equal cosine x minus x. g of zero is
one, g of pi over two is negative pi over

151
00:11:17,591 --> 00:11:19,627
two. By the IVT, yes.

152
00:11:20,766 --> 00:11:25,737
In this lesson, we'll study limits that
involve infinity, in two different ways.

153
00:11:26,866 --> 00:11:32,007
An infinite limit means f of x grows
without bound as x approaches c.

154
00:11:32,457 --> 00:11:37,215
The limit as x approaches zero of one over
x squared is infinity.

155
00:11:38,333 --> 00:11:43,901
If either one-sided limit is infinite, x
equals c is a vertical asymptote.

156
00:11:44,351 --> 00:11:49,918
For one over x minus three, the right
limit is infinity and the left limit is

157
00:11:49,918 --> 00:11:51,220
negative infinity.

158
00:11:52,366 --> 00:11:56,206
A limit at infinity asks what f does as x
grows forever.

159
00:11:56,656 --> 00:12:02,629
The limit as x approaches infinity of two
x squared minus x, over five x squared

160
00:12:02,629 --> 00:12:04,571
plus three, is two fifths.

161
00:12:05,699 --> 00:12:08,409
For large x, only the highest power
matters.

162
00:12:08,859 --> 00:12:15,034
The square root of nine x squared plus x,
over two x plus one, behaves like three x

163
00:12:15,034 --> 00:12:17,861
over two x: the limit is three halves.

164
00:12:18,999 --> 00:12:24,659
A finite limit at infinity gives a
horizontal asymptote. Growth rates also

165
00:12:24,659 --> 00:12:25,271
compare.

166
00:12:25,721 --> 00:12:32,155
Logarithms grow slower than powers, and
powers slower than exponentials. So ln x

167
00:12:32,155 --> 00:12:33,764
over x goes to zero.

168
00:12:34,899 --> 00:12:40,556
Your turn. Find the limit as x approaches
negative infinity of three x plus one,

169
00:12:40,556 --> 00:12:43,667
over the square root of x squared plus
four.

170
00:12:44,117 --> 00:12:45,653
Pause the video and try it.

171
00:12:46,103 --> 00:12:51,382
For negative x, the square root of x
squared is negative x. So it's three x

172
00:12:51,382 --> 00:12:53,634
over negative x: negative three.

173
